SEED_TEXT_DB = {
    "Addendum 1": """Addendum 1: Comprehensive LVP Wear Layer Financial Break-Even Analysis\nTo substantiate the long-term capital expenditure efficiency specified in Section 7, the following financial break-even analysis evaluates 12 mil versus 20 mil luxury vinyl plank (LVP) flooring over a conservative 15-year holding period based on average 2026 industry costs.\n1. Cost & Material Assumptions (Per 1,000 Sq. Ft. Baseline)\nMaterial Pricing: 12 mil LVP material is budgeted at $3.00/sq. ft., while commercial-grade 20 mil LVP material is priced at $3.60/sq. ft., representing a $0.60/sq. ft. upfront material premium.\nConstant Installation & Prep Costs: Professional installation labor ($3.00/sq. ft.), old floor tear-out ($1.50/sq. ft.), and subfloor preparation ($1.50/sq. ft.) remain identical across both tiers at $6.00/sq. ft. combined.\n2. Lifecycle Cost Comparison (15-Year Horizon)\nBecause a 12 mil floor must be completely replaced every 5 years in a rental property due to rapid tenant turnover, pet claws, and rough treatment, it requires three separate installations over a 15-year investment period ($9,000 x 3 = $27,000 total lifecycle cost). In contrast, a commercial-grade 20 mil floor lasts 12 years under the same conditions, requiring only one mid-cycle replacement at Year 12 ($9,600 x 2 = $19,200 total lifecycle cost), with 9 years of residual life remaining at year 15.\n3. Break-Even Point & Return Calculation\nUpfront Cost Premium: The upfront cost premium to buy the 20 mil flooring instead of the 12 mil tier is exactly $600 ($9,600 - $9,000).\nAnnualized Run Rates: 12 Mil = $1,800/yr; 20 Mil = $800/yr. Annual Savings = $1,000/yr.\nBreak-Even Point Formula: Upfront Cost Premium ($600) / Annual Savings ($1,000) = 0.6 Years (approx. 7.2 Months).\nConclusion: The 20 mil flooring breaks even just 7 months into the first tenancy. Everything after that point is pure, preserved rental profit that protects net operating income over the asset lifecycle.""",
    "Addendum 2": """Addendum 2: Shower Enclosures vs. Upgraded Tile Shower Financial & Premium Rental Income Analysis\nTo evaluate the long-term capital expenditure efficiency, tenant retention impact, and rental income premiums across the 24-lot portfolio, the following financial break-even analysis compares standard prefabricated acrylic shower enclosures against premium, custom-tiled upgraded showers over a 15-year holding period.\n1. Cost, Material & Income Assumptions (Per Unit Baseline)\nPrefabricated Acrylic Enclosure: Initial material and professional installation cost is budgeted at $1,200.00 per unit. Standard acrylic units are prone to caulking failures, seam leaks, and bottom-pan flexing in rental environments.\nUpgraded Custom Tile Shower: Initial material, cement backer board, waterproofing membrane, and skilled tile-setting labor total $3,800.00 per unit, representing an upfront cost premium of $2,600.00.\nPremium Rental Income Generation: High-end custom tile showers elevate perceived property value in university-adjacent and workforce rental markets, directly supporting a projected monthly rental premium of $50.00 to $75.00 ($600 to $900 annually per unit).\n2. Lifecycle Cost Comparison & Break-Even\nPrefabricated enclosures require complete rip-out and replacement every 7 years, necessitating two full installations over a 15-year period ($2,600 total). A properly waterproofed custom tile shower lasts the full holding period without structural replacement ($3,800 total). Capturing a modest $65/mo rental premium recovers the $1,400 upfront premium in less than 22 months.""",
    "Addendum 3": """Addendum 3: 2x6 Framing with Closed-Cell Spray Foam vs. 2x4 Framing with Batt Insulation & Louisiana Energy Rebates\nTo substantiate the building envelope and HVAC right-sizing efficiencies specified in Sections 1 and 7, the following financial break-even analysis evaluates standard 2x4 wood-frame construction with fiberglass batt insulation against high-performance 2x6 wood-frame construction with closed-cell spray foam insulation, factoring in Louisiana DEMCO and DNR energy rebates over a 15-year holding period.\n1. Cost, Thermal & Rebate Assumptions (Per Unit Baseline)\nStandard Specification (2x4 Framing + Fiberglass Batt): $6,500.00 per unit (providing R-13 walls/R-19 ceiling and requiring a standard 2.0-ton HVAC system).\nUpgraded Specification (2x6 Framing + Closed-Cell Spray Foam): $11,500.00 per unit ($5,000 upfront construction premium).\nHVAC Right-Sizing Savings: Downsizing from 2.0-ton to 1.5-ton saves $1,500.00 in upfront equipment costs.\nLouisiana Energy Rebates: Leverages up to $8,000 from federal HEEHRA allocations and DEMCO utility rebates ($7,000 to $8,000 capital recovery per unit).\n2. Net Upfront Cost & Annual Utility Savings\nNet Upfront Cost Calculation: Gross upgrade ($5,000) minus HVAC savings ($1,500) minus rebates ($7,000) = -$3,500.00 net initial investment (Immediate Cash Positive Upgrade). Lowering tenant utility burdens by $45-$60 monthly.""",
    "Addendum 4": """Addendum 4: Federal HEEHRA / HEAR Rebate Mechanics & Build-to-Rent Integration\nTo provide complete transparency on the administrative structure and itemized caps governing energy rebates across Wickboldt Capital's portfolio, the following addendum details the federal Home Electrification and Appliance Rebates (HEEHRA) and Louisiana state program mechanics.\n1. Itemized Federal Maximum Incentive Caps (Per Unit)\n- HVAC Heat Pump: Up to $8,000.00\n- Electrical Panel Upgrades: Up to $4,000.00\n- Electrical Wiring Upgrades: Up to $2,500.00\n- Heat Pump Water Heater: Up to $1,750.00\n- Insulation & Air Sealing: Up to $1,600.00\n- Overall Household Limit: Cumulative maximum program cap of $14,000.00 per residential unit.\n2. Build-to-Rent Portfolio Administration: Rental property owners and residential developers are explicitly authorized to access HEEHRA program funds, delivered as immediate point-of-sale discounts.""",
    "Addendum 5": """Addendum 5: Ducted Mini-Split in Conditioned Attic vs. Traditional Split System in Unconditioned Attic\nTo substantiate the superior climate control and mechanical efficiency specified in Sections 1 and 7, the following financial and technical analysis compares a conventional split HVAC system located in a vented, unconditioned attic against an ultra-efficient ducted mini-split heat pump system housed within a fully encapsulated conditioned attic over a 15-year holding period.\n1. Mechanical & Thermal Assumptions: Traditional 3-ton unconditioned attic split system vs. 1.5-ton conditioned attic ducted mini-split system. Eliminates 15%-25% duct thermal losses.\n2. Capital Cost: Traditional = $7,500; Conditioned Attic Mini-Split = $9,200 ($1,700 premium, fully covered by HEEHRA rebates).\n3. Operational Savings: Reduces annual cooling/heating energy consumption by 20%–25%, saving tenants $35–$50 monthly ($540 annually). 3.1-year break-even.""",
    "Addendum 6 & 7": """Addendum 6 & 7: Smart Security Ecosystem & Financial Return Analysis\nTo substantiate the asset management and tenant attraction specifications outlined in Section 7, the following analysis evaluates the integration of a hybrid DIY smart security ecosystem across the 24-lot portfolio.\n1. System Architecture: Standalone smart deadbolts (Schlage/Ultraloq), Ring Video Doorbell ($100-$250), and peel-and-stick wireless entry sensors.\n2. Financial Return & Break-Even: Supports a $15-$30 monthly rent increase and eliminates physical locksmith rekeying fees ($50-$100 per turnover). Hardware investment of $600 per unit breaks even in exactly 24 months, generating $4,500 in cumulative gross rental premiums over 15 years.""",
    "Addendum 8": """Addendum 8: BLDC Ceiling Fan Integration, Energy Offset & Maintenance Analysis\nThis addendum establishes the mandatory integration of high-efficiency Brushless Direct Current (BLDC) ceiling fans in living rooms and bedrooms across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot build-to-rent portfolio.\n1. Airflow Dynamics & Physiological Cooling: BLDC fans create a focused downdraft wind-chill effect, allowing tenants to comfortably raise AC set-points by 2°F to 4°F. Every 1°F increase reduces compressor energy consumption by 3% to 5%.\n2. 15-Year Lifecycle Cost: BLDC option total TCO = $158 vs. Traditional AC fans total TCO = $3,144 (saving $2,986 per home over 15 years).""",
    "Addendum 9": """Addendum 9: High-Durability Commercial Fixtures vs. Standard Builder-Grade Assets (15-Year Maintenance Analysis)\nTo substantiate the long-term maintenance reduction and turnover efficiency specified in Section 7, the following analysis evaluates upgrading key plumbing, hardware, and lighting fixtures from standard builder-grade tier to commercial-grade BTR assets across a 15-year holding period.\n1. Cost Tier Comparison: Total upfront premium for commercial fixtures (solid brass ceramic disc faucets, Grade 2 door hardware, sealed LED lighting) = $750.00 per unit.\n2. Lifecycle Savings: Saves $1,100 per unit in avoided mid-cycle replacements and water leak remediation, achieving full break-even in 3.8 years (45 months).""",
    "Addendum 10": """Addendum 10: Interior Door Selection & Break-Even Financial Analysis for Build-to-Rent Portfolios\nTo support institutional asset longevity and minimize recurring operational friction across Wickboldt Capital’s 24-lot portfolio, the following analysis evaluates standard hollow-core doors against high-durability solid-core engineered wood/MDF doors over a conservative 15-year holding period.\n1. Upfront Cost: Hollow-core doors total ~$450 per unit (requires full replacement every 4-5 years = $1,350 aggregate lifecycle cost). Solid-core MDF doors total ~$1,100 per unit ($650 upfront premium), lasting 15+ years with zero structural replacements.\n2. Break-Even: Fully breaks down its initial cost premium by Year 4, eliminating tenant punch-through liabilities and enhancing acoustic privacy.""",
    "Addendum 11": """Addendum 11: Utilities-Included Rental Strategy & High-Efficiency Utility Delta Financial Analysis\nTo substantiate the revenue optimization and portfolio yield strategies outlined in Section 7, the following analysis evaluates the financial mechanics of offering a utilities-included rental package monetizing the high-efficiency building envelope.\n1. Financial Return: Market baseline utility = ~$190/mo. Actual landlord cost in high-efficiency BTR home = ~$70/mo. Net monthly revenue delta = +$90/mo per unit ($1,080 annual profit per door).\n2. Portfolio Value Expansion: 24 units generate +$25,920 annual NOI, expanding total portfolio market value by +$398,769.23 at a 6.5% cap rate.""",
    "Addendum 12": """Addendum 12: Heat Pump Water Heater Energy Efficiency, Electrical Sizing & Financial Analysis\nTo substantiate the high-efficiency mechanical specifications outlined in Section 7, the following addendum details the energy savings benefits and electrical wire/breaker reductions of a hybrid heat pump water heater (HPWH) compared to traditional electric resistance and standard natural gas water heaters.\n1. Efficiency & Savings: 50-gallon hybrid HPWH achieves 300%-350% efficiency (UEF 3.0-3.5), saving tenants $300 to $450 annually.\n2. Electrical Reduction: Operates on a 15A or 20A double-pole breaker instead of a heavy 30A circuit, allowing copper wiring down-sizing to 14 AWG or 12 AWG.""",
    "Addendum 13": """Addendum 13: All-in-One Heat Pump Washer/Dryer vs. Conventional Electric Dryer & Water Heater Break-Even Analysis\nTo substantiate the advanced appliance and thermal efficiency specifications outlined in Section 7, the following analysis evaluates the performance, operational savings, capital costs, and break-even financial return of an all-in-one ventless heat pump washer/dryer and hybrid heat pump water heater.\n1. Energy Consumption: Uses 50% to 70% less energy (~300-400 kWh/yr vs 1,200 kWh). Ventless design avoids negative pressure envelope pulling.\n2. Savings: Saves tenants $100-$150 annually, compounding with the hybrid water heater to reach $400-$600 total annual tenant utility savings.""",
    "Addendum 14": """Addendum 14: Estimated Construction Costs & Energy Efficiency Rebate Capital Recovery Analysis\nTo provide complete financial transparency regarding capital outlay and federal/utility incentive recovery, the following analysis outlines the estimated per-unit construction costs and active energy rebate capital integration for Wickboldt Capital's build-to-rent homes.\n1. Baseline Cost Breakdown: Total Project Cost (TPC) = $175,350.32 ($152.48 / sq ft). Hard costs = $102,012.74.\n2. Rebate Recovery: Conservative $7,000 to $8,000 cash recovery per unit via federal HEEHRA grants and DEMCO utility programs, lowering net effective unit cost to $167,350 ($145.52/sf).""",
    "Addendum 15": """Addendum 15: Fixed Windows vs. Operable Windows Energy & Cost Savings Analysis\nTo substantiate the building envelope efficiency and long-term operating cost reductions specified in Section 7, the following analysis evaluates the energy savings, thermal performance, and capital cost impacts of specifying fixed (non-operable) windows on all non-bedroom windows versus standard operable windows.\n1. Thermal Performance: Sealed glass units glazed directly into structural framing eliminate air infiltration drafts completely, reducing heating/cooling energy consumption by 5% to 8% ($40-$65 annual utility savings).\n2. Cost Savings: Fixed picture windows cost 20% to 30% less than operable counterparts, saving $300 to $400 upfront per home. Building codes mandate operable windows only in sleeping rooms (bedrooms) for egress.""",
    "Addendum 16": """Addendum 16: Zero-Window Strategy on West Building Facade Energy Savings & Thermal Analysis\nTo substantiate the building envelope efficiency and long-term operating cost reductions specified in Section 7, the following analysis evaluates the thermal performance, solar heat gain coefficient (SHGC) mitigation, and energy savings benefits of omitting windows entirely on the west-facing facade.\n1. Thermal Benefit: Blocks intense late-afternoon solar heat gain (1 PM–7 PM), reducing annual cooling energy consumption by 6% to 10% ($50 to $80 annual savings).\n2. Upfront Hard Cost Savings: Saves $400 to $700 per unit in window materials, framing headers, and finish labor while maximizing tenant privacy.""",
    "Addendum 17": """Addendum 17: Kitchen Exhaust Ventilation & Slim-Line Over-the-Range Microwave Sizing Analysis\nTo substantiate the kitchen ventilation specifications outlined in Section 7, the following analysis evaluates required exhaust airflow capacities (CFM) based on standard engineering formulas, and establishes selection criteria for low-profile over-the-range (OTR) slim-line microwave and vent hood units.\n1. Airflow Sizing: 345 CFM required based on 15 ACH for an 1,150 sq ft home.\n2. Recommendation: Specifying an optimized 400 CFM mid-tier slim microwave achieves code compliance while completely avoiding costly motorized make-up air system mandates (>400 CFM rules).""",
    "Addendum 18": """Addendum 18: Electric Range Selection & Electrical Sizing Analysis\nTo substantiate the appliance standards and electrical rough-in specifications outlined in Section 7, the following analysis evaluates standard electric range selections, electrical load requirements, and operational efficiencies for Wickboldt Capital's build-to-rent homes.\n1. Strategic Advantage: Completely removes natural gas infrastructure connections, meter fees, and interior gas piping. Eliminates combustion byproducts inside airtight spray-foam homes.\n2. Electrical Load: Requires a dedicated 40-Amp or 50-Amp double-pole breaker (240V) with 8 AWG or 6 AWG copper wire, safely accommodated within the 200-Amp main service panel.\n3. Upfront Equipment Cost: Standard 30-inch freestanding electric smooth-top ranges average $550.00 to $800.00 installed.""",
    "Addendum 19": """Addendum 19: 9-Foot Ceilings, Electric Range & 300 CFM Slim-Line Microwave Energy & Clearance Specification\nTo substantiate the architectural, mechanical, and energy-efficiency specifications outlined in Section 7 and Addendum 17, the following analysis evaluates the vertical clearance, spatial volume, make-up air compliance, and energy savings of pairing standard 9-foot ceilings with a 30-inch electric range and an optimized 300 CFM slim-line over-the-range microwave.\n1. Vertical Clearance Advantage: Low-profile slim-line microwaves stand ~10.2 to 10.5 inches tall, providing an extra 5 to 7 inches of bonus clearance above the cooktop compared to 17" traditional units.\n2. Energy Conservation: Capping extraction capacity at 300 CFM satisfies cooking vapor extraction while preventing conditioned air loss and avoiding make-up air mandates.""",
    "Addendum 20": """Addendum 20: Narrow Lot Line & Close House Spacing Mutual Shading Energy Savings Analysis\nTo substantiate the building envelope efficiency and long-term operating cost reductions specified in Section 7, the following analysis evaluates the solar heat gain mitigation and annual cooling energy savings resulting from mutual inter-building shading across narrow lot lines and 16-foot house spacing.\n1. Mutual Shading Mechanics: Two-story homes with 9-foot ceilings completely shade adjacent lower-level walls before 9 AM and after 3 PM year-round.\n2. Cooling Energy Savings: Decreases annual cooling energy consumption by 3% to 5% ($25.00 to $45.00 annual electricity savings).""",
    "Addendum 21": """Addendum 21: Over-Vented Hybrid Roof Assembly: Closed-Cell Foam, EPS Board & LP TechShield Specification\nTo maximize building envelope thermal performance and eliminate long-term moisture and heat accumulation risks in Louisiana’s subtropical climate, Wickboldt Capital specifies an advanced over-vented hybrid roof assembly across the 24-lot build-to-rent portfolio.\n1. Assembly Layers: LP TechShield radiant barrier OSB deck -> 1" continuous EPS rigid foam board -> wood furring strips (creating a continuous ventilation air channel from eaves to ridge vents) -> architectural asphalt shingles. Underside framed tight with closed-cell spray foam.\n2. Financial Return: Upfront construction premium of $600 to $900 per home. Achieves full break-even within 4 to 5 years via 15% to 22% cooling energy reductions ($120-$180 annual utility savings).""",
    "Addendum 22": """Addendum 22: Total Assembly R-Value & Code Compliance Analysis for the Over-Vented Hybrid Roof\nTo verify the energy performance and regulatory compliance of the advanced over-vented hybrid roof assembly specified in Addendum 21, the following analysis calculates the total effective R-value of the system, accounts for the elimination of thermal breaks, and compares the assembly directly against Louisiana and national residential energy code minimums.\n1. Component R-Value: Interior closed-cell spray foam (R-35.75) + continuous 1" EPS board (R-4.5) + LP TechShield OSB deck (R-1.1) = Total effective thermal performance exceeding R-41.5 to R-42.5.\n2. Code Comparison: Comfortably surpasses Louisiana Climate Zone 2 prescriptive ceiling code minimum of R-38 while eliminating thermal bridging through wood rafters.""",
    "Addendum 23": """Addendum 23: 2x6 Exterior Wall Assembly: Open-Cell Spray Foam, LP TechShield & Hardie Siding Specification\nTo substantiate the high-performance wall framing and building envelope specifications outlined in Section 7, the following analysis details the layer-by-layer architecture, vapor management, thermal performance, UV protection, total effective R-value, code compliance, and financial break-even return of the exterior wall assembly for Wickboldt Capital's build-to-rent homes.\n1. Assembly Layers: James Hardie fiber cement siding -> furring strip drainage gap -> LP TechShield taped radiant barrier sheathing -> 2x6 wood studs @ 16" o.c. (monopoly framing) -> open-cell spray foam (R-20 to R-21 cavity). Total effective R-value: R-21.5 to R-22.5 (65% to 73% higher than R-13 code minimum).\n2. Financial Break-Even: Upfront premium of $1,200 to $1,500; simple payback via utility savings of $60 to $90 per year.""",
    "Addendum 24": """Addendum 24: Build-to-Rent Turnover Cost Reduction & Operating Income Impact Analysis\nTo evaluate the operational efficiency and financial returns generated by Wickboldt Capital's high-durability construction standards, the following analysis models the cost of tenant turnovers, quantifies maintenance savings, and demonstrates how saved capital directly increases Net Operating Income (NOI) and cash flow across the 24-lot portfolio.\n1. Traditional Turnover Baseline: $2,500 to $3,400 per event.\n2. BTR Specification Savings: Commercial durability (solid-core doors, 20 mil LVP, smart deadbolts) reduces physical turnover costs to $600 to $800 per event.\n3. NOI & Portfolio Value Impact: Saving $1,500+ per unit annually across 24 units generates +$36,000 annual NOI, expanding total portfolio market valuation by +$553,846.15 at a 6.5% cap rate.""",
    "Addendum 25": """Addendum 25: FORTIFIED Roof™ Standard & Insurance Reduction Strategy\nTo maximize risk mitigation and capture immediate insurance premium reductions across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot portfolio, the following analysis establishes the FORTIFIED Roof™ standard as the optimal cost-effective roofing specification.\n1. Why FORTIFIED Roof is Optimal: Adds only 5% to 15% ($1,000 to $3,500) to standard roof costs while triggering the largest step in insurance savings (~29% discount on the hurricane premium in South Louisiana).\n2. Tiers Compared: Base FORTIFIED Roof provides maximum ROI for rental properties, whereas Silver ($5k-$15k+) and Gold require expensive structural upgrades better suited for luxury primary residences.""",
    "Addendum 26": """Addendum 26: FORTIFIED Roof™ Financial Break-Even & Insurance Savings Analysis\nTo evaluate the financial return on investment of upgrading Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot portfolio to the FORTIFIED Roof™ standard, the following analysis models the upfront installation premium against recurring annual insurance premium reductions in South Louisiana's windstorm market.\n1. Upfront Investment: ~$2,250 per unit.\n2. Annual Insurance Savings: ~$435.00 per year (29% hurricane premium reduction).\n3. Simple Payback: 5.1 Years. 15-year cumulative portfolio savings across 24 units reach $156,600.00.""",
    "Addendum 27": """Addendum 27: Humidity-Sensing Exhaust Fan Switches vs. Timer Switches: Technical Recommendation & Break-Even Analysis\nTo optimize moisture control, eliminate human error, and protect building envelope integrity across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot build-to-rent portfolio, the following addendum revises our ventilation strategy to recommend automatic humidity-sensing (hygrostat) exhaust fan switches over standard manual countdown timers.\n1. The Tenant Compliance Vulnerability: Tenants frequently forget to activate manual timers during showers. Humidity sensors automatically detect RH spikes and run fans until baselines normalize.\n2. Cost & Payback: Upfront hardware cost of $110 to $175 per home (~$40 premium over timers). Payback achieved in 3.1 years via guaranteed mold prevention and motor longevity.""",
    "Addendum 28": """Addendum 28: Roof Overhang, Eave & Vented Over-Roof Engineering Specifications\nTo substantiate the advanced building envelope and structural engineering specifications outlined in Section 7 and Addendums 21–22, this addendum defines the precise roof overhang, eave design, and framing integration for Wickboldt Capital’s two-story, 9-foot wall build-to-rent homes.\n1. Architectural Layout: East-West ridge orientation. 24-inch North/South eave overhangs shade upper walls; 12-inch East/West rake overhangs minimize hurricane uplift.\n2. Structural Details: Continuous rafter tails, monopoly framing integration, hurricane ties (Simpson H10S / H2.5A), and dropped gable framing with 2x4 lookouts.""",
    "Addendum 29": """Addendum 29: Flat Stock Trim vs. Standard Profiled Trim: Detailed Cost Breakdown, Modern Aesthetics & Break-Even Analysis\nTo substantiate interior finish economics, modern architectural alignment, and long-term maintenance efficiency across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot build-to-rent portfolio, the following analysis details the cost comparison, material sourcing, waste reduction, labor efficiencies, and financial break-even return.\n1. Material & Waste Savings: 1x4 flat stock primed MDF saves ~$250 per home (~$300 total package) compared to profiled colonial trim while reducing cut waste from 12% to 5%.\n2. Turnover Maintenance: Monolithic flat surface absorbs impact cleanly and requires zero profile-matching during tenant repainting.""",
    "Addendum 30": """Addendum 30: Color Drenching Design Strategy, Spatial Expansion Mechanics & Room-by-Room Paint Schedule\nTo establish a cohesive, modern, and high-end aesthetic across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot build-to-rent portfolio without incurring excessive customization costs during turnover, the following analysis defines the application of color drenching (painting walls, trim, doors, and ceilings in a single matching color).\n1. Spatial Expansion: Eliminates visual contrast lines and softens corner shadows, making compact footprints feel continuous and expansive.\n2. Sheen Strategy & Colors: Flat ceilings, Eggshell walls, Satin/Semi-gloss trim. Sourced via Lowe's (Sherwin-Williams Infinity Ultra): Accessible Beige (Living/Hall), Alabaster (Bedrooms), Greek Villa (Kitchen), Creamy (Baths).""",
    "Addendum 31": """Addendum 31: Kitchen Backsplash Specification: Ceramic White Subway Tile, Medium Gray Grout, Cabinet & Quartz Integration\nTo optimize upfront capital expenditure while delivering a premium, highly durable aesthetic across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot build-to-rent portfolio, the following addendum defines the standardized kitchen backsplash specification utilizing white ceramic subway tile paired with medium gray grout.\n1. Material Strategy: Ceramic white subway tile with medium gray grout (<$150 material cost per home) provides a clean, boutique-hotel aesthetic that stands up to aggressive turnover cleaning.\n2. Palette Coordination: Paired with soft gray-veined white quartz countertops and color-matched cabinetry.""",
    "Addendum 32": """Addendum 32: Ready-to-Assemble (RTA) Kitchen Cabinet Specification, Supplier Recommendations & Quoting Schedule\nTo establish structural durability, minimize upfront hard-construction costs, and streamline procurement across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot build-to-rent portfolio, the following addendum outlines the standardized Ready-to-Assemble (RTA) kitchen cabinet program.\n1. Engineering Standards: 1/2" to 5/8" select grade-A plywood box construction, dovetail drawer boxes, HDF door panels (humidity-resistant), and soft-close European hardware.\n2. Wholesale Procurement: Sourced via Prime Cabinetry or Buy Wholesale Cabinets (~$4,050 total package per home).""",
    "Addendum 33": """Addendum 33: Closet Organization Systems: Heavy-Duty Steel Track Systems vs. Melamine Laminate Systems\nTo maximize storage durability, minimize installation time, and optimize capital expenditure across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot build-to-rent portfolio, the following addendum defines the standardized closet organization specification utilizing heavy-duty steel track systems.\n1. Steel Track Advantage: Rubbermaid/ClosetMaid heavy-duty steel track systems anchored directly into structural wood studs ($650 fully installed per home).\n2. Comparative Savings: Saves $800 upfront per home compared to fragile particle-board melamine laminate towers while eliminating drywall pull-out liabilities.""",
    "Addendum 34": """Addendum 34: Exterior Siding Specification: Pre-Primed LP SmartSide Engineered Wood vs. Unpainted James Hardie Fiber Cement\nTo optimize upfront capital expenditure, accelerate construction velocity, and balance long-term maintenance profiles across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot build-to-rent portfolio, the following addendum defines the standardized exterior siding selection.\n1. Material Comparison: Pre-primed LP SmartSide engineered wood achieves complete weather sealing in a streamlined 2-coat paint application. Raw James Hardie fiber cement requires a heavy 3-coat schedule.\n2. Capital Savings: Specifying LP SmartSide saves $4,800 per home ($115,200 portfolio-wide across 24 lots).""",
    "Addendum 35": """Addendum 35: Interior Door Hardware & Finish Specifications: Grade 2 Commercial-Duty vs. Grade 3 Contractor Packs\nTo optimize operational longevity, eliminate tenant lockout and mechanism failure liabilities, and standardize finish hardware across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot build-to-rent portfolio, the following addendum defines the standardized interior door knob specification.\n1. Hardware Standard: ANSI Grade 2 heavy-duty passage and privacy door hardware (Schlage Georgian / Kwikset Juno) with coin-slot emergency releases (~$210 per home).\n2. Reliability: Eliminates Grade 3 spring failures, loose screw posts, and tenant lockout friction.""",
    "Addendum 36": """Addendum 36: Recommended Wholesale Trade Suppliers for General Contractors\nTo streamline material procurement, secure volume-based contractor tier pricing, and ensure rapid job-site delivery across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot build-to-rent development, the following addendum establishes the master list of authorized wholesale trade suppliers and commercial distribution counters.\n1. Master Directory: Prime Cabinetry (Cabinets), Rubbermaid Pro (Closets), LP Direct / ABC Supply (Siding), Schlage Commercial (Hardware), Carrier Enterprise (HVAC), Sherwin-Williams Commercial (Paint).\n2. Protocols: All orders must route through established commercial contractor accounts to lock in tier-1 volume discounts.""",
    "Addendum 37": """Addendum 37: LED Wafer Light Specifications & Authorized Wafer Light Supplier Directory\nTo standardize electrical rough-ins, enhance modern interior aesthetics, and streamline specialized procurement across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot build-to-rent portfolio (optimized for 1,150 sq. ft. two-story homes), the following addendum defines the mandatory LED wafer light standards.\n1. Size & Specs: 4-inch IC-rated airtight LED wafer lights featuring selectable CCT switches (2700K-5000K) and wet-location ratings for bathrooms and kitchens.\n2. Suppliers: Sourced via Amico or Sunco high-volume multi-family contractor packs.""",
    "Addendum 38": """Addendum 38: Window Treatment & Blind Specifications for Build-to-Rent Portfolios\nTo standardize interior finishes, maximize durability across tenant turnovers, and maintain a uniform aesthetic across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot build-to-rent portfolio, the following addendum establishes the mandatory window covering specifications.\n1. Blind Spec: 1-inch cordless faux wood blinds in a strict white colorway.\n2. Benefits: Complete moisture resistance in baths/kitchens, enhanced child safety, zero tangled strings during tenant turnovers, and uniform exterior curb appeal.""",
    "Addendum 39": """Addendum 39: Closet Door Specifications: Solid-Core Swing Doors vs. Bifold Doors\nTo eliminate chronic maintenance vulnerabilities, prevent tenant turnover damage, and maximize long-term asset durability across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot build-to-rent portfolio, the following addendum establishes the mandatory closet door specification.\n1. Prohibition: Bifold doors are strictly prohibited due to track bending and pivot pin dislodgement.\n2. Mandate: Solid-core swing doors (double or single) with 3 heavy-duty hinges and magnetic catches to eliminate mechanical breakdowns.""",
    "Addendum 40": """Addendum 40: New Build Subterranean Termite Prevention, Treatment Specifications & Wholesale Procurement\nTo establish a rigorous, multi-layered defense against subterranean termites, protect structural framing, and comply with regional high-risk mandates across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot build-to-rent portfolio, the following addendum defines mandatory pre-construction treatments.\n1. Soil Treatment: Professional non-repellent liquid termiticide (Termidor SC / Taurus SC @ 0.06% active ingredient) applied to soil and footings.\n2. Wood Treatment: Structural framing treated with borate solutions (Bora-Care 1:1 ratio) to secure lifetime framing protection against wood-boring pests, backed by a 5-year contractor warranty.""",
    "Addendum 41": """Addendum 41: 30-Inch Stem Wall Construction & Integrated Foam Brick Ledge Specifications\nTo establish standardized engineering practices, structural concrete guidelines, and formwork protocols for 30-inch-tall stem walls across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot build-to-rent portfolio, the following addendum details assembly steps.\n1. Reinforcement: #4 rebar vertical dowels and three continuous horizontal lines of #4 rebar.\n2. Brick Ledge: High-density EPS foam block-outs secured against the upper interior face of forms to cast a precise 4" x 12" brick ledge shelf for masonry veneer.""",
    "Addendum 42": """Addendum 42: Fixed-Expense Rental Rate Sensitivity Analysis ($1,500 to $1,700 Gross Potential Rent Matrix)\nTo evaluate portfolio cash flow expansion, cash-on-cash yield growth, and debt service coverage ratio (DSCR) scaling under varying rental rate scenarios across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot build-to-rent master development, this addendum establishes the definitive financial sensitivity matrix.\n1. Core Rules: Fixed OpEx ($427.50/mo), 5% vacancy, 25% equity ($43,837 basis), permanent debt service $831.62/mo ($131,512 loan at 6.50% / 30-yr).\n2. Yield Expansion: Scaling rent from $1,500 to $1,700 more than doubles annual cash-on-cash returns from 4.54% to 9.74% while expanding DSCR from 1.20 to 1.43.""",
    "Addendum 43": """Addendum 43: Appraisal Value Sensitivity Analysis (Increased Appraised Values in $5,000 Increments)\nTo evaluate the portfolio impact of higher finished appraisal valuations upon completion of vertical construction, this addendum models the refinancing mechanics as appraised values increase in $5,000 increments starting from the baseline appraised finished value of $175,350.32 up to $215,350.32.\n1. Refinancing Mechanics: Evaluates 75% LTV permanent loans as appraisals rise from $175k to $215k.\n2. Liquidity vs. Cash Flow: Shows cash-out liquidity extraction up to $30,000 per door, balanced against increased debt service compressing immediate operating cash flow.""",
    "Addendum 44": """Addendum 44: Maximum Appraisal Value Matrix for 1.20 DSCR ($50 Rental Increments from $1,500 to $1,800)\nTo determine the maximum finished appraisal valuation that can support a 75% LTV permanent loan while strictly maintaining Wickboldt Capital’s institutional underwriting target of a 1.20 Debt Service Coverage Ratio (DSCR), this addendum models rent increases in $50 monthly increments from $1,500.00 up to $1,800.00 Gross Potential Rent (GPR).\n1. Mathematical Correlation: Proves that every $50 monthly rent increase raises borrowing capacity by ~$6,260 in loan principal, supporting an additional $8,346 in appraisal valuation at 1.20 DSCR.\n2. Max Cap: Pushing rent to $1,800 supports a $225,352 finished appraisal valuation per door.""",
    "Addendum 45": """Addendum 45: Cash-Out Refinancing Potential Matrix ($50 Rental Increments from $1,500 to $1,800)\nTo evaluate the capital extraction and liquidity potential across Wickboldt Capital's 24-lot master development, this addendum models the cash-out refinancing proceeds generated at each $50 monthly rental increment.\n1. Cash-Out Mechanics: Holding baseline build cost flat at $175,350, growing rental income expands NOI and maximum supportable loan sizing.\n2. Portfolio Liquidity: Capturing top-of-market rents unlocks up to $37,501 per unit in tax-free cash-out refinancing proceeds, totaling $900,047 portfolio-wide across 24 lots.""",
    "Addendum 46": """Addendum 46: Phase Two Infrastructure Capital Sizing Matrix ($300,000 Road Construction Funding Analysis)\nTo evaluate how Wickboldt Capital can fully fund the $300,000.00 infrastructure capital requirement for Phase Two road construction (14 building lots) using cash-out refinancing proceeds generated by Phase One (10 building lots), this addendum calculates the exact per-unit rental rate.\n1. Capital Sizing: $300k infrastructure requirement / 10 Phase One lots = $30,000 cash-out extraction per door.\n2. Required Rent: Proves that achieving a stabilized rental rate of $1,740 across Phase One unlocks exactly $300,000 in cash-out proceeds at permanent refinancing, fully funding Phase Two road construction with zero outside equity.""",
    "Addendum 47": """ADDENDUM 47: UNDERWRITING OPERATING EXPENSE RATIO (OER) BENCHMARK\nProperty Location: Hammond, Louisiana (Tangipahoa Parish)\nSubject Asset: New-Build Single-Family Residential Rental Portfolio (3-Bedroom, 2-Bathroom High-Efficiency Configuration)\nEffective Date: August 2026\n1. Purpose & Scope\nThis Addendum establishes the standardized operational underwriting parameters for the subject new-build rental portfolio. Pursuant to institutional underwriting requirements for ground-up construction in Tangipahoa Parish, the Operating Expense Ratio (OER) is strictly fixed at 30.0% of Gross Operating Income (GOI).\nThis model explicitly excludes vacancy factors and below-the-line debt service financing costs to isolate direct, stabilized asset-level operational performance.\n2. Standardized Pro Forma Operating Budget\nBased on a normalized baseline gross market rent of $1,500.00 per month ($18,000.00 annually) per unit, the operational expenditure breakdown under the 30% OER constraint is defined as follows:\nFinancial Metric / Expense Category | Percentage of Gross Rent | Annual Cost (USD) | Monthly Cost (USD)\nGross Operating Income (GOI) | 100.0% | $18,000.00 | $1,500.00\nProperty Taxes (Tangipahoa Parish / City of Hammond) | 5.5% | $990.00 | $82.50\nProperty & Casualty Insurance (Wind/Hail/Hazard) | 11.5% | $2,070.00 | $172.50\nProfessional Property Management (10% Collected) | 10.0% | $1,800.00 | $150.00\nMaintenance & Capital Expenditure (CapEx) Reserves | 3.0% | $540.00 | $45.00\nTotal Operating Expenses (OpEx) | 30.0% | $5,400.00 | $450.00\nNet Operating Income (NOI) | 70.0% | $12,600.00 | $1,050.00\n3. Underwriting Assumptions & Directives\nInsurance Mitigation: The 11.5% insurance allocation assumes utilization of high-efficiency, wind-rated, and resilient building methodologies.\nWarranty Offset for Reserves: The reduced 3.0% combined maintenance and CapEx reserve is validated strictly by active manufacturer and builder warranties on brand-new mechanical systems and roofs.""",
    "Addendum 48": """ADDENDUM 48: CASH-OUT REFINANCE & INTEREST RATE BUY-DOWN MATRIX\nProperty Location: Hammond, Louisiana (Tangipahoa Parish)\nSubject Asset: New-Build Single-Family Residential Rental Portfolio\nEffective Date: August 2026\n1. Purpose & Scope\nThis Addendum establishes the institutional underwriting parameters and net liquidity outcomes for executing a cash-out refinance on stabilized new-build single-family rentals in Hammond, Louisiana.\n2. Core Underwriting Constants\nBaseline Construction Loan to Pay Off: $125,000.00\nTotal Sponsor Equity Position (25% LTC): $41,666.67\nGross Potential Rent (GPR): $1,500.00 / month ($18,000.00 annually)\nVacancy Factor (5%): -$75.00 / month (-$900.00 annually)\nEffective Gross Income (EGI): $1,425.00 / month ($17,100.00 annually)\nOperating Expenses (30% OER of EGI): $427.50 / month ($5,130.00 annually)\nNet Operating Income (NOI): $997.50 / month ($11,970.00 annually)\nTarget DSCR: 1.20x | Max Allowable Annual Debt Service: $9,975.00 ($831.25 / month)\n3. Final Net Cash-Out Refinance Matrix (Post-Point Deductions)\nBaseline (7.00%): $548.97 Net Cash-Out (0 Points)\nTier 1 (6.75% / -0.25%): $2,592.58 Net Cash-Out (1 Point / $1,288.81 cost)\nTier 2 (6.50% / -0.50%): $4,735.31 Net Cash-Out (2 Points / $2,647.66 cost)\nTier 3 (6.25% / -0.75%): $6,984.31 Net Cash-Out (3 Points / $4,081.99 cost)\nTier 4 (6.00% / -1.00%): $9,346.69 Net Cash-Out (4 Points / $5,597.78 cost)\nTier 5 (5.75% / -1.25%): $11,829.96 Net Cash-Out (5 Points / $7,201.58 cost)\nTier 6 (5.50% / -1.50%): $14,441.80 Net Cash-Out (6 Points / $8,900.54 cost)\nTier 7 (5.25% / -1.75%): $17,190.19 Net Cash-Out (7 Points / $10,702.49 cost)\nTier 8 (5.00% / -2.00%): $20,083.46 Net Cash-Out (8 Points / $12,615.95 cost)\n4. Directives: Recommends executing Tier 4 (6.00% Interest Rate / 4 Points), capturing $9,346.69 in net cash-out proceeds per unit while locking in long-term stability at the maximum allowable monthly debt service ceiling of $831.25.""",
    "Addendum 49": """ADDENDUM 49: MARKET APPRAISAL VS. MODEL BASIS COMPARISON MATRIX\nProperty Location: Hammond, Louisiana (Tangipahoa Parish)\nSubject Asset: New-Build Single-Family Residential Rental Portfolio\nEffective Date: August 2026\n1. Purpose & Scope: Establishes empirical correlation between market appraisal values and rental rates in Hammond, LA, comparing regional market valuation benchmarks directly against the established development cost basis.\n2. Core Benchmarks: Monthly Gross Rent = $1,500.00; Model Total Project Cost = $166,666.67; NOI = $997.50/mo.\n3. Valuation Spread Comparison:\n- Model Cost Basis: $166,666.67 (25% equity + 75% loan).\n- Market Appraisal (GRM Approach - 11.5x to 12.5x Gross Rent): $207,000.00 to $225,000.00 (+$40,333 to +$58,333 variance).\n- Market Appraisal (Income Approach - 6.0% to 6.5% Cap Rate): $184,000.00 to $199,500.00.\n- Blended Expected Market Appraisal: ~$205,000.00 (+$38,333.33 instant equity creation achieved solely through ground-up development efficiency).""",
    "Addendum 50": """ADDENDUM 50: SECTION 8 PAYMENT STANDARDS & VALUATION FLOOR Analysis\nProperty Location: Hammond, Louisiana (Tangipahoa Parish)\nSubject Asset: New-Build Single-Family Residential Rental Portfolio\nEffective Date: August 2026\n1. Purpose & Scope: Evaluates HUD Fair Market Rent (FMR) Section 8 Housing Choice Voucher payment standards against private market rents in Hammond, LA.\n2. Benchmarks: 3-bed FMRs anchor within $1,300 to $1,450/mo. Model baseline of $1,500/mo sits slightly above standard baseline Section 8 limits, positioning the high-efficiency new build at the upper tier of the voucher spectrum or private-pay professional market.\n3. Valuation Floor: Section 8 FMRs establish a reliable government-backed revenue floor, securing NOI against macroeconomic fluctuations while maintaining a $38,333 equity buffer against a ~$205,000 appraisal value.""",
    "Addendum 51": """ADDENDUM 51: SECTION 8 PAYMENT STANDARDS & REAL ESTATE VALUATION CORRELATION ANALYSIS\nProperty Location: Hammond, Louisiana (Tangipahoa Parish)\nSubject Asset: New-Build Single-Family Residential Rental Portfolio\nEffective Date: August 2026\n1. Purpose & Scope: Evaluates HUD Fair Market Rent (FMR) Section 8 Housing Choice Voucher payment standards against private market rents and real estate appraisal values in Hammond, Louisiana.\n2. Dual-Market Compatibility: Lenders view dual-market compatibility (private-pay and Section 8) favorably, broadening the prospective tenant pool and mitigating long-term vacancy risk below the modeled 5.0% factor.""",
    "Addendum 52": """ADDENDUM 52: TO PROJECT DEVELOPMENT SPECIFICATIONS\nProject Name: Wickboldt Capital – Build-to-Rent Residential Portfolio\nLocation: Hammond, Louisiana (RS-3 Zoning District)\nSubject: Site Plan, Zoning Compliance, and Architectural Footprint Finalization (Tracts / 75-Foot Deep Lot Profile)\nDate: August 2026\n1. Purpose of Addendum: Formally establishes revised, 100% code-compliant architectural and site planning parameters for single-family residential development on restricted 75-foot deep parcels within City of Hammond RS-3 zoning.\n2. Final Approved Site Dimensions & Setbacks:\n- Parcel Width: 40.0 Feet | Parcel Depth: 75.0 Feet\n- Front Yard Setback: 20.0 Feet (19 ft open air accounting for roof overhang)\n- Rear Yard Setback: 15.0 Feet (14 ft open air accounting for roof overhang)\n- Side Yard Setbacks: 5.0 Feet per side\n3. Architectural Building Envelope: 28.0 Feet Width x 40.0 Feet Length exterior framing. 12-inch roof overhangs. Gross building area: 1,120 Square Feet (incorporating an integrated 10' x 20' single-car garage yielding 200 SF, alongside an optimized 2-bed, 2-bath living program).\n4. Compliance: Fully compliant with City of Hammond UDC Article 6 standards. Variance Requirement: None.""",
    "Addendum 53": """ADDENDUM 53: PROJECT DEVELOPMENT SPECIFICATIONS\nProject Name: Wickboldt Capital – Build-to-Rent Residential Portfolio\nLocation: Hammond, Louisiana (Zone AE / RS-3 Zoning District)\nSubject: Foundation Engineering, Flood Compliance, Brick Ledge Details, Driveway Fill Grading, Culvert Specifications, Apron Standards, Driveway Width, Slope Transition, and Side Swale Drainage Addendum\nDate: August 2026\n1. Mandatory Elevation Benchmarks (NAVD 88):\n- Front Property Line / Road Ditch Baseline: Elevation 40.0'\n- Rear Property Line (Swale Invert Start): Elevation 40.75' (1% positive drainage slope)\n- FEMA Base Flood Elevation (BFE): Elevation 42.0'\n- Design Finished Floor Elevation (FFE): Elevation 43.0' (Includes 1.0' mandatory freeboard; 3.0-foot vertical foundation lift above natural grade).\n2. Foundation & Structural Methodology: Reinforced concrete perimeter stem walls with foam block integration to form a structural 4" x 12" brick ledge. Uniform FFE of 43.0' across the 28' x 40' footprint (including 200 SF garage).\n3. Driveway & Drainage: 20-foot wide driveway with 15% engineered fill slope transition. Roadside culvert: One (1) 30-foot joint of 24-inch Galvanized Corrugated Steel Pipe with Asphalt Coating. Side swale ditches maintain continuous 1.0% positive slope outfall.""",
    "WC-SOP-001": """WC-SOP-001: Master Document Control Procedure & Numbering System\nDefines the standard operating procedure for nomenclature, version control, digital archival, and administrative review governing all corporate governance procedures, engineering specifications, and financial addendums across Wickboldt Capital's development portfolio.""",
    "WC-SOP-002": """WC-SOP-002: Portal User Access Control & Administrator Security Policy\nEstablishes institutional security protocols, role-based access control (RBAC), multi-tier routing permissions, and database credentialing standards to protect proprietary financial proformas and project data across the portal.""",
    "WC-SOP-003": """WC-SOP-003: User Registration, Account Management & Backend Security Procedure\nDefines backend database procedures for user onboarding, account password updates, session state persistence, and audit logging across all multi-user modules."""
}

